<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.8.2: Identifying the Most Useful Form of an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00187</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.2: Identifying the Most Useful Form of an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0d61741b-2b14-49e7-a92b-6a05d7865b72</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>For numbers 1 - 4, use the situation below:</para>
<para><quote>
    After a parade, a group of volunteers is helping to pick up the trash along a 2-mile stretch of a road. The group
    decides to divide the length of the road so that each volunteer is responsible for cleaning up equal-length
    sections.
  </quote><quote>Find the length of a road section for each volunteer if there are the following numbers of volunteers. Be prepared to show your reasoning.
  </quote></para><figure><media alt="Mummers' Parade in Philly."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dfb7838fc9d86de04e46523624735a8692051722"/></media></figure><!--Q1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>8 volunteers</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( \frac14 \) mile</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>10 volunteers</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( \frac15 \) mile</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>25 volunteers</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( \frac {2}{25} \) mile or 0.08 mile</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>36 volunteers</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( \frac{2}{36} \) or \( \frac{1}{18} \) mile</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation that would make it easy to find \( l \), the length of a road section in miles for each
        volunteer, if there are \( n \) volunteers.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( l = \frac{2}{n} \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For numbers 6 - 9, find the number of volunteers in the group if each volunteer cleans up a section of the following
  lengths. Be prepared to show your reasoning.
</para>
<!--Q6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>0.4 mile</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n=\frac{2}{0.4}=\) 5 volunteers</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        \( \frac {2}{7} \) mile
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n=\frac2{\displaystyle\frac27}=\frac{2}{1}\times\frac{7}{2}=\) 7 volunteers</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>0.125 mile</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n=\frac{2}{0.125}=\) 16 volunteers</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>
        \( \frac {6}{45} \) mile
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(n=\frac2{\displaystyle\frac{6}{45}}=\frac{2}{1} \times \frac{45}{6}=\) 15 volunteers</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation that would make it easy to find the number of volunteers, \( n \), if each volunteer cleans up a
        section that is \( l \) miles. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( n = \frac{2}{ l} \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


