    </div>
    
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q3-->

<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>
        What do you think the graph looks like when \(x\) is between 0 and \(\frac12\)? Continue to use the graphing tool or
        technology outside the course to graph the function
        \(y=\frac{2}{x}\) with your points. Try some values of \(x\) to test your idea. (Students were provided access to Desmos.)

      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example:</para>
    <para>The graph gets steeper and steeper as \(x\) gets closer to 0.</para>
    <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bba34b4119919710e25d5a614d6d772cf523e636"/></media></figure></para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q4-->

<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>
        What is the largest value \(y\) can ever be?
      </para>
    <para>\(y=\frac{2}{x}\) with your points. Try some values of \(x\) to test your idea. In Desmos, zoom in on your graph.</para>
    <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example:</para>
    <para>There is no limit to the value of 𝑦.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
